Then I’d stop treating the rainbow as a display and treat it as an operator.

Not:

\text{light} \rightarrow \text{rainbow} \rightarrow \text{observer learns something}

but:

\text{light} + \text{water} + \text{geometry}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{rainbow operator}}
\rightarrow
\text{world is changed downstream}

A few possibilities become interesting.

A rainbow could be a spectral sorter. White sunlight enters a field of droplets and different wavelengths leave along different trajectories. Usually we say that merely lets us see the colors, but physically it has also redistributed photon direction, wavelength composition, and polarization. Imagine organisms, materials, or atmospheric chemistry positioned to exploit those locally enriched spectral bands. Then the rainbow becomes a temporary energy-routing structure.

More strangely, it could function as a selector. Different molecules respond to different wavelengths. If the rainbow spatially separates wavelengths, then instead of every location receiving approximately the same spectral mixture, different locations briefly receive different recipes:

x \mapsto \lambda(x)

Now chemistry, photosynthesis, photoreceptors, insect behavior, or microbial activity could respond differently across that field. The “pretty colors” would be the visible trace of a much larger spectral gradient doing work.

It could also be a polarization machine. Rainbow light has characteristic polarization. Humans barely notice polarization directly, but many animals can detect it. To such an organism, the rainbow might not merely be colored—it could be a giant transient orientation field, navigation marker, mating cue, or contrast amplifier.

And then there’s the ecological version: perhaps the rainbow is a broadcast event. Rain + sun + atmospheric clearing reliably means certain things about humidity, temperature, water availability, insect emergence, plant surfaces, and impending weather. An organism doesn’t need to understand optics. Evolution could simply discover:

\text{rainbow-pattern detected}
\Rightarrow
\text{change behavior}

At that point the rainbow genuinely does something because it closes a causal loop:

\text{atmosphere}
\rightarrow
\text{rainbow}
\rightarrow
\text{perception}
\rightarrow
\text{behavior}
\rightarrow
\text{ecological consequence}

But I think the more interesting imaginative leap is this:

What if prisms are not just decomposers, but routers?

The same operation that reveals hidden components also sends those components somewhere different.

That generalizes far beyond light:

\boxed{\text{Prism Operator: mixture → separate components → redirect components}}

A cochlea does this to sound. A chromatography column does it to molecules. A mass spectrometer does it to ions. A market does it to demand. An immune system does it to molecular patterns. Attention does it to information.

Then the rainbow belongs to a family of machines whose hidden second function is:

separation creates the possibility of differential action.

Before the rainbow:

R+G+B+\cdots

are bound together.

After the rainbow:

R\rightarrow A,\qquad
G\rightarrow B,\qquad
B\rightarrow C

Now each component can encounter something different.

So in the photograph you sent, imagine that arc not as paint laid over the storm, but as a temporary atmospheric switchboard: the storm constructs the medium, the Sun supplies the carrier, geometry performs the routing, and the colored arc is merely the part of the machinery your eyes happen to expose.

That would make the rainbow not the message.

It would be the visible exhaust of a routing operation.